{
 "id": "stonehenge",
 "name": "Stonehenge and its landscape",
 "subtitle": "From the first earthworks to the Jacobean antiquaries, 4000 BCE to 1625 CE",
 "scopeNote": "A 6.4 by 5 km piece of the Stonehenge World Heritage landscape, from 4000 BCE to 1625 CE, with the sarsen circle at the origin. Two levels of detail: the whole landscape is a schematic overview (barrows, cursus, henges, hillfort and town are placed from mapped outlines and published dates, but their form is generic), while the 'district' round the stones (about 800 by 650 m) has stone-by-stone megaliths from a catalogue, the ditch and bank, the Aubrey Holes, the first stretch of the Avenue and the Heel Stone. No feature is shown before its date, and where a date is a range or a model the landmark says so. The state of the stones before the first drawings (de Heere, 1570s) is mostly undated: which stones stood, leant or lay in 1625 is inferred, not recorded. Height comes from a modern LIDAR surface with modern works removed, with the ditch and Avenue ditches cut back in on their dates. Ground cover, trees and house positions are reconstruction. The solstice episodes mark the sunrise and sunset directions with overlay lines and discs; the scene's one fixed sun is not moved to the solstice. Amesbury's outline and the Durrington settlement area are schematic envelopes, not surveyed boundaries.",
 "origin": {
  "lat": 51.17888,
  "lon": -1.82619,
  "note": "FINAL. Centre of the sarsen circle to within ~3 m (geography research: circle fitted to OSM-mapped uprights 51.178859, -1.826208; EH 2010 survey NGR SU 12244219; the stone catalogue's fitted centre 51.178868, -1.8261962). Megalith landmarks are anchored at the catalogue's fitted centre. Local x = metres east, z = metres south."
 },
