  "note": "Heights are metres above a local datum of Ordnance Datum minus 62 m (stones about 39.5, the Avon floor about 0 to 2: the engine clamps land at 2 m). Analytic fallback: a least-squares fit of the engine's own terrain formula (base + tilt + 12 gaussian hills - 6 valleys) to 1,302 EU-DEM 25 m samples, with the 2 m of datum difference removed from the base (hills and valleys are relative). Error against the DEM: RMS 4.5 m over the whole area (3.1 m, max 6.9 m, within 500 m of the stones); the two DEMs themselves disagree by RMS 2.6 m. It is a broad-shape model, not a survey. Where terrain-lidar.json is present the Environment Agency 1 m bare-earth LIDAR (datum offset 62) replaces this relief and only the dated ditches below are cut into it. The Stonehenge ditch is a closed 72-point ring of radius 53 m (a ditch centreline taken from the monument's c. 110 m overall diameter; the ditch width and profile are not recorded in the sources read), 1.5 m deep, dug about 3000 to 2900 BCE (the model switches it on in 3001 to 2995 BCE). Its silting (0.35 filled by 2400 BCE, 0.6 by 1500 BCE, 0.8 by 1625 CE) is MODELLED, not measured: the real ditch was also re-cut (Stage 3) and its later fill is not dated here. The two Avenue ditches (23 m apart, from the OpenStreetMap-mapped centreline) are dug 2400 to 2380 BCE in the model and 70% silted by 1625: the Avenue's date is 2430 to 2200 BCE (Darvill 2012) or 2345 to 2200 BCE (English Heritage antler dates), and the ditches were later ploughed nearly flat, so the depth shown is a gentle stand-in for ditches about 2 m deep when new.",
